{
  "id": "COMPOUND:40609 - PROTEIN:6340",
  "verbalization": "Calyxamine A is implicated in the regulation of the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex through its interaction with Histone deacetylase 5, which is a key component of the polycomb repressive complex 2 (PRC2). This relationship is supported by multiple pathways, all of which point to Histone deacetylase 5 as a central mediator. The evidence suggests that Calyxamine A may modulate the activity of Histone deacetylase 5, thereby influencing the function of the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex. The presence of multiple targets, including 4H-1-Benzopyran-4-one and Sarcandrolide D, further reinforces the notion that Calyxamine A acts through a shared mechanism, potentially targeting a common downstream pathway. The integration of these findings highlights a possible role for Calyxamine A in the regulation of histone modification, which is critical for gene silencing and cellular differentiation. The mechanism appears to be consistent across different molecular entities, suggesting a broader impact on the PRC2 complex.",
